            "text": "An elegant and thorough analysis is presented in the frozen cold report. Below is the structural verification of every explicit claim, mapped directly to the governing authority (`CORE_SPEC_DRAFT_0_15_CONSOLIDATED_CANDIDATE.md`) and the test fixture (`experiments/004/Q004_S03_VARIABLE_OWNERSHIP.isg`).\n\n---\n\n### 1. Structural Verification of Case #1\n*   **Claim 1.1 (Variable Occurrences):** Exactly one occurrence of `?0` on the LHS of `?0 =\u003e 10`.\n    *   *Verification:* **VERIFIED**. The serialized term in `^6301` is `[ ?0 =\u003e 10 ]`. There is a single occurrence of `?0` in this subcase.\n*   **Claim 1.2 (Ownership):** `?0` is unowned. Implication (`=\u003e`) does not introduce variable ownership.\n    *   *Verification:* **VERIFIED**. Section 11 states: *\"Declarative constructors do not introduce variable ownership by themselves: A =\u003e B ...\"* No quantifier, abstraction, or interface binder is present in Case #1 to own the variable.\n*   **Claim 1.3 (Well-Formedness):** The term is invalid under its asserted closed contract.\n    *   *Verification:* **VERIFIED**. Since `^6301` asserts a closed body, and Section 11 dictates that *\"A standalone object asserted to be a closed declarative proposition is invalid if any variable remains unowned/free\"*, the presence of the unowned `?0` renders this term invalid.\n*   **Claim 1.4 (No Implicit Semantics):** `?0` does not receive implicit universal, existential, or wildcard/pattern meanings.\n    *   *Verification:* **VERIFIED**. Section 11 states: *\"There is no implicit universal quantification, existential quantification, or wildcard/pattern interpretation merely because `?n` appears in a declarative surface.\"*\n\n---\n\n### 2. Structural Verification of Case #2\n*   **Claim 2.1 (Variable Occurrences):** Two occurrences of `?0` (one binder, one usage in the edge).\n    *   *Verification:* **VERIFIED**. The term is `*?0 (?0 10)`. The variable `?0` occurs both as the bound variable